\title{Function types}
\import{prelude}

\p{An element of a function type \code{A -> B} assigns for each element of \code{A}, an element of \code{B}.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} AccompaniedTour := \kw{sig}
  Child : Set
  Adult : Set
  accompanying-adult : Child -> Adult
\kw{end}
}

\p{If we have \code{m : AccompaniedTour}, then \code{m.accompanying-adult} is a function that sends elements of \code{m.Child} to elements of \code{m.Adult}.}

\p{In most programming languages that contain function types, they are thought of as \em{procedures} for producing an output given an input. In Coln, a record field with function type is ultimately stored as a table that associates an output to each possible input. Coln also outputs constraints that ensure that this table really does behave as a function: each possible input is associated with one output.}

% Instance Example

\p{These can certainly use the same collection on both sides.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} BoatRide := \kw{sig}
  Stop : Set
  next : Stop -> Stop
\kw{end}
}

\p{Each stop has a next stop. The final stop can map to itself, representing the boat remaining at the landing.}

% Instance Example

\p{But that is not all functions can do. Though there are some restrictions (see [[Levels]]), the input and output types of a function can be general types, and not just those that we have declared as fields. We can do the following:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} TourSchedule := \kw{sig}
  Departure : Set
  year : Departure -> Int
  Child : Departure -> Set
  Adult : Departure -> Set
\kw{end}
}

% Instance Example

\p{Here the \code{Child} and \code{Adult} fields are \em{families} of collections, indexed by the elements of the collection \code{Departure}. In this case, we are saying that \code{Child} is not a single \code{Set}, but a separate \code{Set} for each element of \code{Departure}.}

\p{Coln is nice and compositional, so rather than writing out \code{Child} and \code{Adult} as new, separate fields in \code{TourSchedule}, we might prefer to re-use the theory \code{Tour} that we already defined:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} TourSchedule := \kw{sig}
  Departure : Set
  year : Departure -> Int
  participants : Departure -> Tour
\kw{end}
}

% Instance Example, or skip if the same

\collapsedaside{We cannot ask whether the same child participates in two departures, because each departure has a separate collection of children. To track children across departures, we would need \em{one} shared set of children, and a relation recording which departures each child participates in. This can be accomplished with [relations](001G).}

\p{To apply a function to an argument, we juxtapose the two. For example, we might want to specify a sweet on a particular floor:}

\pre{
\kw{theory} FactoryFloors := \kw{sig}
  Sweet-on-floor : Int -> Set
  everlasting-gobstopper : Sweet-on-floor 3
\kw{end}
}

% Instance Example

\p{To write a function that takes multiple arguments, the preferred method in Coln is to use the [curried](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Currying) style. That is, a function accepting both an \code{Int} and a \code{String} is written as \code{Int -> (String -> Result)}. We associate function arrows to the \em{right}, so that we can skip the parentheses in this case. For example:}

\pre{
\kw{theory} FactoryStock := \kw{sig}
  Sweet-on-floor-with-flavour : Int -> String -> Set
\kw{end}
}

% Instance Example

\p{Conceptually, applying \code{Sweet-on-floor-with-flavour} to an integer gives back a function that awaits a string. Providing that string, we finally get back a collection of sweets. This repeated application is still done by juxtaposition: we might write \code{(Sweet-on-floor-with-flavour 3) "chocolate"}. Function application associates to the \em{left}, so again we can leave off the parentheses and write \code{Sweet-on-floor-with-flavour 3 "chocolate"}.}

\p{As a database, \code{Sweet-on-floor-with-flavour} is ultimately lowered to a table with two columns, one for the floor and one for the flavour. Each row ID represents a sweet in the collection specified by those two values.}

\collapsedaside{It is also possible to define a function that accepts a genuine pair as an argument, but we defer the syntax for this to \mvrnote{TODO}.}
